  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Cyanide may interact with the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14 through a series of target relationships, with the High-affinity choline transporter acting as a common intermediary. This pathway is supported by multiple independent evidence streams, each showing that Cyanide targets the choline transporter, which in turn is targeted by different compounds (Plantago seed, 24-Methylenecycloartanone, and Lethedioside B), all of which ultimately target the 26S proteasome subunit. The consistent scores across these paths (final_score=0.5181) indicate a plausible, though not fully understood, mechanism by which Cyanide may influence the proteasome. The exact nature of the interaction remains unclear, but the shared target of the choline transporter suggests a potential regulatory role for Cyanide in modulating proteasome activity. The evidence supports a possible mechanism involving the choline transporter as a central node in the interaction network, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct involvement of Cyanide in the regulation of this subunit."
